					<item>
				<guid>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-407817#post-1915470</guid>
				<title>(no title)</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-407817/annon#post-1915470</link>
				<description></description>
				<pubDate>Sun, 15 Dec 2013 00:33:34 +0000</pubDate>
				<wikidot:authorName>Doctor GE Smith</wikidot:authorName>				<wikidot:authorUserId>1786409</wikidot:authorUserId>				<content:encoded>
					<![CDATA[
						 <p>First off, enthusiastic upvotes to both tales!<br /> While I honestly like a <em>little</em> LOLfoundation to lighten the mood (akin to the joking comparison of wounds before the beast attacks in that one classic film which I am totally not spoilering), I've noticed a lot of people who feel the &quot;old guard&quot; of researcher characters have gotten OP. While there obviously is no canon, this tale can fill a lot of people's head-canon as promoting most of those characters out of the story (as one often does with high-level PCs in some tabletop RPGs).<br /> Also, despite this half of the story being suggested to be less dark, consider that the previous O5s imposed stability along with their decried stagnation. A nearly full O5 turnover to <em>these characters</em> (no offence intended) might well end up extremely grimdark for low level researchers. Also keep in mind that O5s are written out of the memories of the rest of the Foundation. The gaps these characters would leave - hell, the gaps from Clef alone - could easily cause psychological dissonance and side effects.</p> <p>The open field this tale leaves is impressive. Thank you.</p> 
				 	]]>
				</content:encoded>							</item>
